#!lm
MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:compnov:bible.genesis.vulgate:42.2.t0-urn:cts:compnov:bible.genesis.vulgate:42.2.t22
REASONING="audivi" is the independent verb of the first clause, governing a "quod" clause (Late Latin substitute for indirect statement, treated as a dependent clause): "quod triticum venundetur in aegypto" — "venundetur" is a dependent passive verb, with "triticum" as its subject and "in aegypto" as an adverbial prepositional phrase. Then two coordinated imperatives: "descendite" (independent, intransitive) and "emite" (independent, transitive active), joined by "et" (t9), which relates the two verbs. "nobis" is a dative with "emite"; "necessaria" (substantive adjective) is its direct object. "ut possimus vivere" is a purpose clause: "ut" is the subordinating conjunction depending on "emite"; "possimus" is the dependent verb (intransitive), with "vivere" as a complementary infinitive (not its own verbal expression). "et non consumamur inopia" is coordinated with "possimus" under the same "ut": "consumamur" is dependent, transitive passive, with "non" adverbial and "inopia" ablative. The "et" (t18) joins the two dependent verbs.

